WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:05.640
All right, so what is converge?

00:05.680 --> 00:06.320
It's very simple.

00:06.320 --> 00:09.480
It's going to go and change the value to a different type.

00:09.480 --> 00:12.920
And as well at the same time it helps formatting the value.

00:12.960 --> 00:13.200
Okay.

00:13.240 --> 00:14.920
So let's check the syntax of the convert.

00:14.920 --> 00:16.040
And it looks like this.

00:16.080 --> 00:20.840
It starts with the function convert and it accepts two parameters the data type.

00:20.880 --> 00:21.400
First.

00:21.440 --> 00:25.160
Since we can use this function in order to cast the data types.

00:25.160 --> 00:28.560
So you can use string, integer, date and so on.

00:28.560 --> 00:30.560
And then we have to specify the value.

00:30.560 --> 00:32.760
So which value should be casted.

00:32.760 --> 00:38.840
And the last parameter it is optional one where you define the style the format of the value.

00:38.880 --> 00:40.360
Let's have this very simple example.

00:40.360 --> 00:46.480
We are saying convert to the data type integer int and the value that should be converted is one, two,

00:46.520 --> 00:47.680
three as a string.

00:47.680 --> 00:49.800
So it's going to convert it to integer.

00:49.840 --> 00:55.160
We are saying convert to a varchar and the value that should be converted is the order date.

00:55.160 --> 00:57.240
So the order date should be a date.

00:57.240 --> 01:03.640
So we're going to convert it from date to Varchar using the format or the style of 34.

01:03.680 --> 01:07.500
So here we are specifying a style a format for this value.

01:07.500 --> 01:08.820
And of course it is optional.

01:08.820 --> 01:14.060
And if you are not using anything, the default value that's going to be used is zero.

01:14.060 --> 01:16.900
So this is the syntax of the convert in SQL.

01:16.940 --> 01:17.260
All right.

01:17.260 --> 01:20.300
So now we're gonna have a few examples on how to work with the convert.

01:20.300 --> 01:23.780
So let's go and convert for example string to integer.

01:23.780 --> 01:26.020
So we're going to say for example convert.

01:26.180 --> 01:27.620
So what is the target data type.

01:27.620 --> 01:30.100
It's going to be the integer and the value.

01:30.140 --> 01:32.300
It's going to be like for example 123.

01:32.300 --> 01:37.220
So and let's call it like this string to integer.

01:37.220 --> 01:38.940
And the function is convert.

01:38.940 --> 01:41.580
So now in the column name as you can see I'm using here brackets.

01:41.580 --> 01:44.740
And that's because I'm using like empty spaces and so on.

01:44.740 --> 01:47.580
And with that I will get more freedom on how to name things.

01:47.580 --> 01:48.980
So this is just the name.

01:48.980 --> 01:51.020
So this is no function or something.

01:51.220 --> 01:52.620
Let's go and execute it.

01:52.660 --> 01:53.940
Now it's going to see it's going to work.

01:53.940 --> 01:57.620
So we are converting from a string value to an integer.

01:57.980 --> 02:00.940
And the output this 123 here is not a string.

02:00.980 --> 02:03.020
This is the data type of integer.

02:03.060 --> 02:03.460
All right.

02:03.460 --> 02:08.020
So now let's have another example where we want to convert from string to date.

02:08.070 --> 02:11.550
So the target is going to be the date and the value.

02:11.870 --> 02:13.350
Let's have this value as usual.

02:14.030 --> 02:20.230
And we're going to go and call it string to date convert.

02:20.750 --> 02:21.070
Okay.

02:21.110 --> 02:22.430
So let's go and execute it.

02:22.430 --> 02:26.670
Now in the output we will get this information this string as a date.

02:26.670 --> 02:29.550
And with that we have converted the data type from string to dates.

02:29.590 --> 02:33.630
Now let's have another example where we want to convert the date time to a dates.

02:33.630 --> 02:38.190
As you remember the creation time is a date time and we would like to have it as only date.

02:38.550 --> 02:42.710
So let's go and convert and we would like it to be as real date.

02:42.710 --> 02:48.270
But this time it's going to be a column called Creation Time and let's give it a name.

02:49.110 --> 02:53.110
So we are converting date time to date.

02:53.390 --> 02:55.270
But of course here we have to go and select.

02:55.270 --> 02:58.110
So from sales orders.

02:58.270 --> 02:58.830
That's it.

02:58.830 --> 03:00.430
Let's go and execute it.

03:00.470 --> 03:06.070
Now as you can see in the output we got only date I'm going to go and select the creation time in the

03:06.110 --> 03:07.110
query as well.

03:07.150 --> 03:10.150
So now as you can see the creation time was before a date time.

03:10.150 --> 03:12.330
So we have that time information as well.

03:12.330 --> 03:16.090
But if you go and cast it using the convert and make it only.

03:16.130 --> 03:18.450
Date so SQL can go and convert it to date.

03:18.450 --> 03:21.010
And you're going to lose all the information about the time.

03:21.010 --> 03:23.610
So so far what we are doing here is just casting.

03:23.610 --> 03:26.450
So we are changing the data type from one to another.

03:26.450 --> 03:28.330
But in the convert we can do both.

03:28.330 --> 03:30.130
We can do casting and formatting.

03:30.130 --> 03:31.210
So let's see how we can do that.

03:31.210 --> 03:34.330
I will just get rid of those information at the start.

03:34.330 --> 03:35.530
So creation time.

03:35.930 --> 03:42.290
And now we're going to go and convert the date time of the creation time to a varchar to a string.

03:42.290 --> 03:45.530
And as well to give it the format of the USA standard format.

03:45.570 --> 03:46.970
So let's see how we can do that.

03:47.010 --> 03:48.610
We're going to start with convert.

03:48.850 --> 03:51.170
We are changing now to varchar.

03:51.210 --> 03:55.690
So this is the new data type and the value is the creation time.

03:55.730 --> 03:58.690
And now if I don't give it a style it's going to stay with the standard format.

03:58.690 --> 04:01.650
But we would like to have the USA standards.

04:01.650 --> 04:04.890
So in order to do that we're going to go and add the style of the format.

04:04.890 --> 04:06.970
So it's going to be 32.

04:07.530 --> 04:08.650
So that's it.

04:08.690 --> 04:10.690
Let's have a name like this.

04:11.290 --> 04:17.630
So USA standard and we are using the style of 32.

04:17.670 --> 04:18.430
Let's go with that.

04:18.430 --> 04:20.710
This is just a name again, so it's not a function.

04:20.710 --> 04:21.830
Let's go and execute it.

04:21.870 --> 04:23.670
And now in the output we got a new field.

04:23.670 --> 04:26.150
And the data type of this field is a varchar.

04:26.150 --> 04:27.950
So it's not a date or date time.

04:27.950 --> 04:31.910
And as you can see the date now is formatted using this style.

04:31.910 --> 04:33.910
The 32 the USA standard formats.

04:33.910 --> 04:37.190
So it starts with a month then a day and then a year.

04:37.190 --> 04:41.750
So now let's go and do the same thing in order to get the standard format in Europe.

04:41.750 --> 04:44.190
So I will just go and copy the whole thing.

04:44.590 --> 04:46.110
I will just change the style.

04:46.510 --> 04:49.790
So instead of 32 we're going to go with the 34.

04:49.910 --> 04:52.270
And I will just change the name as well.

04:52.750 --> 04:55.830
So so we are just changing the style.

04:55.830 --> 04:57.190
Let's go and execute it.

04:57.190 --> 04:57.390
Now.

04:57.390 --> 05:00.190
As you can see, we got the same thing we have as well a varchar.

05:00.430 --> 05:02.110
And the format now is different.

05:02.110 --> 05:05.750
So we have here the day then the month and then the year.

05:05.750 --> 05:07.910
So this is how you work with the convert function.

05:07.910 --> 05:14.270
You can use it in order to do only casting or not only that you can do casting and as well formatting.

05:14.270 --> 05:16.190
So you have both things in one function.

05:20.160 --> 05:25.040
And if you're talking about which styles are available, we have many styles that you can use inside

05:25.040 --> 05:25.800
the convert.

05:25.800 --> 05:29.800
So I have prepared for you a list of all styles that you can use with the convert.

05:29.800 --> 05:36.800
So we have styles only for the dates and other styles only for the time and styles for only date time.

05:36.800 --> 05:41.440
Now in the download folders you can find here one file called all Culture Formats.

05:41.440 --> 05:46.880
And here you can find one query that I've prepared where you can find inside it the different cultures

05:46.880 --> 05:47.840
and examples.

05:47.840 --> 05:49.040
So let's go and copy it.

05:49.040 --> 05:52.800
And let's go back to SQL, paste it and let's see the results.

05:52.800 --> 05:56.560
So now if you check the output we got the first column is the cultures that is used.

05:56.560 --> 05:58.960
So we have a lot of cultures like around 17.

05:58.960 --> 06:03.680
And you can see how the numbers are formatted or the date is formatted based on this culture.

06:03.680 --> 06:04.320
So it's really fun.

06:04.320 --> 06:10.400
You can check here, for example, how the format in Japan or Korea or France and the German one, if

06:10.400 --> 06:13.960
you scroll down you can find the Arabic, the Russian and so on.

06:13.960 --> 06:18.440
So you can see the formats of each date is changing based on the culture.

